	abstract = {Voice phishing (vishing) has emerged as an increasingly pervasive global cybersecurity threat, with South Korea experiencing particularly severe financial and societal impacts. In response, this study proposes a novel detection framework grounded in agentic artificial intelligence (AI). Unlike conventional static detection systems, the proposed framework incorporates autonomous learning and adaptive decision-making capabilities, enabling real-time responses to evolving and adversarial attack strategies. The agentic architecture further promotes modularity, automation, and intelligent behavior, offering enhanced flexibility, scalability, and resilience.To evaluate the effectiveness of this approach, we conducted extensive experiments on the KorCCVi v2 dataset, leveraging a diverse set of small language models (SLMs) available via the Ollama framework as well as machine learning-based classifiers. The results demonstrate that models like qwen2.5:14b achieve an F1-score of 0.975, highlighting the strong potential of these lightweight models in accurately detecting voice phishing attempts under dynamic conditions. Collectively, our findings underscore the viability of agentic AI as a transformative paradigm for building robust and adaptive voice phishing detection systems.  © 2025 IEEE.},
