	affiliations = {Heilbronn University of Applied Sciences, Faculty of Business, Max-Planck-Straße 39, Heilbronn, DE-74081, Germany},
	abstract = {Phishing and identity manipulation via audiovisual channels, particularly in videoconferencing, have become significant cybersecurity threats. Advances in artificial intelligence (AI) enable attackers to convincingly mimic voices and appearances, exploiting human vulnerabilities. This paper examines the mechanisms behind such attacks, focusing on deepfake technologies used for financial fraud, political disinformation, and social engineering. Our experimental results reveal that over 70% of participants were unable to detect manipulated content, highlighting the limitations of human perception. The study identifies real-time detection tools, awareness campaigns, and organizational measures as key protective strategies. By combining technical and legal approaches, this research aims to develop effective solutions to counter these threats and maintain trust in digital communication. © 2025 Gesellschaft fur Informatik (GI). All rights reserved.},
